# Suppresses one ThreadSanitizer report in the child of one fork test.
#
# The child of fork() initializes event_loop_slot_table.rwlock again instead
# of unlocking it: glibc's rwlock records the thread ID of its writer, and the
# one thread of the child has a new ID, so a plain unlock there releases
# nothing (see _cthreadcomm_atfork_release_impl in src/cthreadcomm.c). The
# init itself produces no report: _ccol_rw_lock_reinit_in_child() of
# src/common.c tells ThreadSanitizer that the lock is released before it
# initializes it again.
#
# What remains is a "thread leak" that ThreadSanitizer reports when the child
# of fork_safety.fork_does_not_inherit_a_write_locked_event_loop_slot_table
# exits. The test starts a "holder" thread that write-locks the slot table
# across the fork(), and the parent joins that thread unconditionally, before
# every REQUIRE_* that can return early. fork() copies only the calling
# thread, so the holder never exists in the child, and the child never joins
# it; ThreadSanitizer still counts the record of the thread that the child
# inherited. Without this file the suite gives exactly this one report, with
# GCC and with Clang.
#
# The anchor is the test function, the only frame of the "created by" stack
# that is always resolved: a bare pthread_create has no frame of this library
# to anchor on. A wider anchor could hide a real thread leak elsewhere in this
# suite.
thread:_TAU_TEST_FUNC_fork_safety_fork_does_not_inherit_a_write_locked_event_loop_slot_table
